\title{ROpenEigen: R Bindings for Eigen 3}
\description{
A thin, object-oriented interface to the Eigen 3 C++ template library.
}
\section{Two kinds of object}{


\code{eigenMat} holds a dense matrix on the C++ side. It behaves like a base R
matrix for the idioms that matter: \code{A \%*\% B}, \code{t(A)}, \code{solve(A, b)}, \code{A[i, j]},
\code{A + 1}, \code{abs(A)}, \code{sum(A)}, \code{Re(A)}, \code{dim(A)}. Use \code{\link[=as.matrix]{as.matrix()}} to bring the
data back to R.

Decomposition objects (\code{eigenLLT}, \code{eigenLU}, \code{eigenSVD}, ...) hold a
factorisation that is computed once and reused. Their methods are reached
with \code{$}: \code{llt$solve(b)}, \code{svd$singular_values()}. \code{names(obj)} lists them.
}

\section{Cost model}{


Crossing the R/C++ boundary copies once, in the direction you asked for.
Every operation after that runs entirely inside Eigen. This is the opposite
trade-off from a zero-copy view: it makes R's copy-on-modify semantics hold,
at the price of one copy at the edge.
}

\section{What is deliberately missing}{


Integer matrices. Eigen's \code{MatrixXi} and \code{MatrixXd} are distinct types that do
not interoperate, so keeping integers would mean writing every operation
twice. Integer input is promoted to double, as \verb{RcppEigen::as<MatrixXd>}
does.

Sparse matrices are not wrapped yet, although the headers are installed.
}

